\page basics Basics

VesperCSI is a Channel State Information capture tool designed to work extremely fast to enable edge-device signal processing.
The internals of FeitCSI are completely redesigned and written from scratch. While enhancing the already existing features, VesperCSI
also allows anyone to extend the functionality of the tool with plugins.

We will start with the most basic usage.

---

## Capturing CSI Traffic

- The most basic usage of the VesperCSI is as follows:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff
```

- The `-i` (or `--interface`) option specifies which interface to use for capturing CSI. This is the only obligatory command-line option.
- Interface is specified with permanent MAC address of the interface.
- Issuing the command this way has literally no effect. Saves no output, performs no action, just captures and ditches the CSI data.

> VesperCSI needs root privileges to work.


---

## Configuring the Interface

- Frequency, format, and channel width can be specified by the following syntax:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --frequency 5180 --format VHT --channel-width 80
```

- By default, VesperCSI operates on frequency `2412` and `HT` format, and channel width of 20.
- These parameters are set by the VesperCSI program via Netlink commands.


---

## Logging 

- VesperCSI outputs relevant information about what is being perfomed in the background.
- If one desires to save the log output to a file:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --log-file hello.log
```

- To increase the verbosity of the log output:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --verbose
```

- Verbosity level can be set with the optinal argument to `--verbose` option.

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --verbose=2
```

- To suppress all log output to the screen/file:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --quiet
```

> Warning: The most optimal method to capture high rate CSI data is to eliminate unnecessary CPU time of other operations, especially the ones caused
> by slow I/O operations. Use `--quiet` mode for maximum performance.


---

## Saving CSI Output

- The captured CSI data can be written to a file:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --out-file mycsi.dat
```

---

## Filtering

- By default, VesperCSI tries to match the frequency, format, and channel width to count it as a successful capture.
- To remove any filtering on format and channel width:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --unrestricted
```

> Frequency depends on the interface configuration.

- VesperCSI captures all kinds of traffic coming from different kinds of devices. 
- To force the VesperCSI to only capture a specific CSI data that is coming from a specific device:

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --source-mac 00:11:22:33:44:55
```

> This operation is performed on user-space, meaning that there is no kernel or hardware level optimization to ignore packets based on
> their source MAC addresses. This option drops a CSI data immediatelly if it does not match to the one specified with `--source-mac`.


---

## Plugins

- Functionality of VesperCSI tool can be extended with the help of plugins.
- If you have a plugin with `.vesper` extension, you can make the tool load that plugin:


```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --plugin myplugin.vesper
```

> Plugins are meant to be standalone work units. Whenever a CSI data is captured, it is also redirected to the callback function of
> the plugin. It is plugin's responsibility to handle the CSI data.


---

## Configuration File

- VesperCSI takes a command-line option called `--config-file` to configure it's internal working mechanism (\ref advanced). 

```shell
vespercsi -i aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff --config-file vespercsi.conf
```

---
